## Proof

**Claim.** The intersection of the kernels of all irreducible characters of $G$ that contain $H$ equals $H$ itself.

---

**Lemma.** *For any finite group $\Gamma$, $\bigcap_{\psi \in \mathrm{Irr}(\Gamma)} \ker(\psi) = \{e\}$.*

*Proof of Lemma.* Let $g \in \bigcap_{\psi} \ker(\psi)$, so $\psi(g) = \psi(1)$ for every irreducible character $\psi$. Let $\rho$ denote the character of the (left) regular representation of $\Gamma$. We have
$$\rho = \sum_{\psi \in \mathrm{Irr}(\Gamma)} \psi(1)\,\psi,$$
so
$$\rho(g) = \sum_{\psi} \psi(1)\,\psi(g) = \sum_{\psi} \psi(1)^2 = |\Gamma|.$$
But the regular character satisfies $\rho(g) = 0$ for $g \neq e$ and $\rho(e) = |\Gamma|$. Hence $g = e$. $\square$

---

**Main proof.** Let $\pi: G \to G/H$ be the canonical quotient homomorphism. We use the standard correspondence:

> *The irreducible characters of $G$ whose kernel contains $H$ are exactly the inflations of the irreducible characters of $G/H$.*

Indeed, if $\chi \in \mathrm{Irr}(G)$ and $H \subseteq \ker(\chi)$, then $\chi$ is constant on cosets of $H$, so it factors through $\pi$: there is a class function $\bar\chi$ on $G/H$ with $\chi = \bar\chi \circ \pi$. Since
$$\langle \bar\chi, \bar\chi\rangle_{G/H} = \frac{1}{|G/H|}\sum_{\bar g \in G/H} |\bar\chi(\bar g)|^2 = \frac{1}{|G|}\sum_{g \in G} |\chi(g)|^2 = \langle \chi,\chi\rangle_G = 1,$$
$\bar\chi$ is irreducible. Conversely, the inflation $\widetilde{\psi} = \psi \circ \pi$ of any $\psi \in \mathrm{Irr}(G/H)$ is irreducible (same inner-product argument) and satisfies $H \subseteq \ker(\widetilde{\psi})$ since $\pi(H) = e_{G/H} \in \ker(\psi)$.

Moreover, $\ker(\widetilde{\psi}) = \pi^{-1}(\ker(\psi))$. Therefore:

$$\bigcap_{\substack{\chi \in \mathrm{Irr}(G)\\ H \subseteq \ker(\chi)}} \ker(\chi) \;=\; \bigcap_{\psi \in \mathrm{Irr}(G/H)} \pi^{-1}(\ker(\psi)) \;=\; \pi^{-1}\!\left(\bigcap_{\psi \in \mathrm{Irr}(G/H)} \ker(\psi)\right).$$

By the Lemma applied to $\Gamma = G/H$, the inner intersection is $\{e_{G/H}\}$. Hence

$$\pi^{-1}(\{e_{G/H}\}) = H.$$

---

**Conclusion.** The intersection of the kernels of all irreducible characters of $G$ that contain the normal subgroup $H$ is precisely $H$:

$$\boxed{\displaystyle\bigcap_{\substack{\chi \in \mathrm{Irr}(G)\\ H \subseteq \ker(\chi)}} \ker(\chi) \;=\; H.}$$

